    This server attempts to implement the Minecraft server protocol for alpha
    version 1.0.15.  Pretty old!  Modern versions of Java have a little bit of
    trouble with this client, running the client with the following JVM option
    seems to get it working just fine.

      -Djava.util.Arrays.useLegacyMergeSort=true

    This file contains all my notes about the protocol as I assembled them. I
    am doing this entirely from a clean room as a learning process, I am not
    looking up the protocol on the internet as that would be too easy.

    Everything seems to use network byte order, big endian. Packets do not
    specify their lengths, so an unknown packet ID is irrecoverable which has
    downsides and benefits.


On Connect

    On connect, the client sends packet 0x01 followed by packet 0x0d, a full
    positional update. This position appears to be the same each time,
    containing the position for center of the central chunk.


Types

  byte

    Unsigned byte

  i8

    Single-byte byte signed integer

  i16

    Two-byte signed integer, big-endian

  i32

    Four-byte signed integer, big endian

  i64

    Eight-byte signed integer, big endian

  f32

    Four byte floating point number, IEEE 754

  f64

    Eight byte floating point number, IEEE 754

  f27_5

    Four byte fixed point decimal number; 27 integer bits, 5 fractional bits

  bool

    Single byte, value is either 0 or 1

  string     length:i16 bytes:byte[length]

    UTF-8 string, max length INT16_MAX

  eid

    4-byte integer entity ID

Coordinate Space

    The coordinate space in Minecraft is Z-forward and Y-up.

    Minecraft operates under three separate coordinate systems. The first is
    world coordinates, which are a triplet of (f64, f64, f64). The second is
    block coordinates, which is a triplet of (i32, i16, i32). The last one is
    chunk coordinates, which is a duplet of (i32, i32).

    Sometimes, world coordinates are packed as a triplet of
    (f27_5, f27_5, f27_5), these are called 'entity coordinates' from now on.

Client Packets

    These packets sent by the client to the server. There appears to be no
    packet length field, so unknown packets are irrecoverable for our server.

  Handshake Initiation (0x01) -- at least 11 bytes

    Sent by the client upon connection, appears to be the client identifying
    itself to the server.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        i32(?)    unknown           0x0000000d
        string    username          (min 3, max 16 chars)
        string    password          defaults to 'Password'

      Example packet

        01
        00 00 00 0d
        00 07
        50 6c 61 79 65 72 33        "Player3"
        00 08
        50 61 73 73 77 6f 72 64     "Password"


  Player Grounded (0x0a)

    Indicates whether the player is standing/falling.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        bool      grounded          true if grounded

      Example packet

        0a
        01

      Payload size

        1 byte

  Player Position (0x0b) -- 33 bytes

    Sent by the client when the player moves.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        f64       x-position
        f64       y-position
        f64       head y-position
        f64       z-position
        bool      grounded          true if grounded

      Example packet

        0b
        40 1a 00 00 00 00 00 00
        40 50 32 fd b7 51 1b 96
        40 50 9a ab cb d1 1b 96
        40 88 74 00 00 00 00 00
        00


  Player Rotation (0x0c) -- 9 bytes

    Sent by the client when the player rotates around.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        f32       body rotation     in degrees
        f32        head pitch        in degrees
        bool      grounded          true if grounded

      Example packet

        0c
        bf ac cc cd
        00 00 00 00
        01


  Player Full Position (0x0d) -- 41 bytes

    Combined 0x0a 0x0b 0x0c packet, full positional update.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        f64       x-position
        f64       y-position
        f64       head y-position
        f64       z-position
        f32       rotation
        f32       head pitch
        bool      grounded          true if grounded

      Example packet

        0d
        40 21 00 00 00 00 00 00
        40 50 40 00 00 00 00 00
        40 50 a7 ae 14 80 00 00
        40 21 00 00 00 00 00 00
        c3 34 00 00
        00 00 00 00
        00


  Disconnect (0xff) -- at least 2 bytes

    Sent when the client disconnects.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        string    reason            defaults to "Quitting"

      Example packet

        ff
        00 08
        51 75 69  74 74 69 6e 67    "Quitting"


Server Packets

  Keep-Alive (0x00) -- 0 bytes

    Keep alive packet, no payload.

      Example packet

        00


  Authentication (0x01)

    Sent to the client in response to their authentication packet.  Meaning of
    fields entirely unknown as of now, but presumably the player's entityID.

      Example packet

        01
        00 00 00 00
        00 00 00 00


  Message (0x03)

    Sent to display a message in the client chat window.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        string    message

      Example packet

        03
        00 1d
        c2 a7 65 50 6c 61 79 65 72 36 35 36 20 6a 6f 69     ..ePlayer656 joi
        6e 65 64 20 74 68 65 20 67 61 6d 65 2e              ned the game.


  Player Full Position (0x0d) -- 41 bytes

    Combined 0x0a 0x0b 0x0c packet, full positional update.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        double    x-position
        double    head y-position   !! inverted from client !!
        double    y-position        !! inverted from client !!
        double    z-position
        float     rotation
        float     head pitch
        bool      grounded          true if grounded

      Example packet

        0d
        40 21 00 00 00 00 00 00
        40 50 40 00 00 00 00 00
        40 50 a7 ae 14 80 00 00
        40 21 00 00 00 00 00 00
        c3 34 00 00
        00 00 00 00
        00


  Receive Item (0x11) -- 5 bytes

    Sent to the client when the player receives an item.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        i16       item id
        i8        stack count
        i16       durability

      Example Packet

        11
        00 03
        01
        00 00


  Spawn Player (0x14)

    Sent when a player joins the game.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        eid       entity id
        string    entity name
        f27_5     x
        f27_5     y
        f27_5     z
        i8        yaw
        i8        pitch
        i16       held item

      Example packet

        14
        00 00 01 24
        00 09
        50 6c 61 79 65 72 35 35 30       "Player550"
        ff ff cd 90
        00 00 08 73
        ff ff d9 b0
        00
        00
        00 00


  Spawn Item (0x15) -- 22 bytes payload

    Informs the client about a new item.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        eid       entity id
        i16       item ID
        i8        stack count
        f27_5     x
        f27_5     y
        f27_5     z
        i8        yaw
        i8        pitch
        i8        unknown

      Example packet

        15
        00 00 09 DB
        00 06
        01
        FF FF FB 9C
        00 00 08 44
        00 00 66 CE
        00 00 00


  Entity Picked Up? (0x16) -- 8 bytes

    Two entity IDs, first is the entity being picked up (seems to always be an
    entity ID), the second is the entity receiving the picked up entity.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        eid
        eid       receiver

      Example Packet

        16
        00 00 01 c3
        00 00 01 35


  Entity Destroyed (0x1D) -- 4 bytes

    Notifies the client of the end of an entity's lifetime.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        eid       entity id         entity being destroyed

      Example Packet

        1d
        00 00 01 c3


  Entity Alive (0x1E) -- 4 bytes

    Notifies the client that an entity still exists.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        eid       entity id

      Example packet

        1E
        00 00 09 DB


  Entity Move (0x1F) -- 7 bytes

    A relative move from the entity's last known position

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        eid       entity id
        i8        x                 divide by 32
        i8        y                 divide by 32
        i8        z                 divide by 32

      Example packet

        1f
        00 00 01 c3
        f8
        f1
        06


  Entity Full Position (0x22) -- 18 bytes

    Fixed precision entity position and rotation.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        eid       entityID
        f27_5     x
        f27_5     y
        f27_5     z
        i8        rotation
        i8        rotation

      Example packet

        00 00 0f 47
        ff ff e2 cd
        00 00 08 04
        00 00 74 3a
        f9
        00


  Chunk (0x32)

    Sent to the client when it has to load or unload a chunk. If a chunk is
    loaded, the data for it arrives later in 0x33.

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        i32       chunk x
        i32       chunk z
        bool      load              false to unload, true to load

      Example packet

        32
        ff ff ff f6
        00 00 00 27
        01


  Chunk Data (0x33) -- at least 17

    Sends the client an update to a chunk. It sends block coordinates for
    the origin of the update, followed by exte

      Fields

        type      value             notes
        i32       origin x
        i16       origin y
        i32       origin z
        i8        width
        i8        height
        i8        depth
        i32       size              compressed size
        byte[]    data              zlib compressed

      Example packet

        33
        00 00 00 00
        00 00
        00 00 03 00
        0F
        7F
        0F
        00 00 13 D5
        78 01 ED 9D 4D 72 E4 C6 11 85 49 D3 11 B4 62
        D4 6A 86 4F E0 08 5F 42 57 98 AD 6F E5 2B F8
        0A DE F8 00 5E .. .. .. .. .. .. .. .. .. ..


  Unknown (0x34) -- at least 10 bytes

    Probably related to chunks, given packet ID and proximity to other chunk
